{
  "id": "arcas-robin",
  "source": "NASA's half-scale Arcas Robin (short, configuration 1) and bioscience Arcas (long, configuration 2) wind-tunnel models: J. C. Ferris, NASA TN D-4013 (1967), Mach 0.60 to 1.20, Langley 8-foot transonic pressure tunnel; C. D. Babb and D. E. Fuller, NASA TN D-4014 (1967), Mach 1.50 to 4.63, Langley Unitary Plan wind tunnel. Reynolds number 9.8e6 per metre, transition fixed by grit. US Government works.",
  "reading": "Read from the reports' plots, which print no tables, on 600-dpi renders with each plot's grid calibrated locally (2026-09-18, M1.8a). C_N at each plotted symbol (fins at 0 deg, roll 0): +-0.01 (TN D-4014 Fig. 5), +-0.013 (Fig. 6), +-0.02 (TN D-4013 Figs. 7(b) and 9(b), a coarse grid); the report states +-0.03 for C_N itself. Where roll angles 0, 22.5 and 45 deg overlap in TN D-4013, the roll-0 square where it can be told apart, else the cluster's centre. CP in percent of body length from the nose tip, read at the test Mach numbers from faired curves: +-0.3% (+-0.6% at Mach 0.95 and 1.00 on the short model, +-0.5% for configuration 2 below Mach 2.3). TN D-4014's Fig. 7 states no fin cant; C_m over C_N from Fig. 5(b) (fins at 0 deg) reproduces it within 1.2% of body length. Checked: the short model's CP at Mach 2.96 from those slopes, 67.0%, against Fig. 7's 66.98%.",
  "geometry": {
    "note": "Model dimensions from TN D-4014 Fig. 1(a) (inches; page 10 of the PDF), which TN D-4013 Fig. 1 (calibers) matches within about 3%. Stations in inches from the nose tip.",
